# WHAT THIS FILE IS: the answers upstream llama.cpp gave for this checkpoint, on this
# machine's own Metal device. Unlike a fixture this port captured of itself, it can show
# that this port is wrong rather than only that it changed. The values are
# log-probabilities over the full vocabulary, because that is the only quantity
# llama-server exposes; a replay converts its own logits the same way before comparing.
# The prompt is the raw prompt file, no chat template, sent as text and tokenized by the
# reference with its own add_special. That prepends `leading_token` above, which is the
# model's bos_token_id as its config.json declares it, and it is why this fixture grades
# the regime the model is actually run in rather than a state it never sees. The sidecar
# tokenizer.json does NOT add it - its post-processor is `single: [$A]` with no special
# tokens - so a replay has to prepend the id itself, and the per-case tokens_digest is
# over the sequence WITH it.
# WHEN IT FAILS: this port disagrees with an independent implementation of the same
# checkpoint. Name the case and the diverging rank, dump that row, and walk the ops.
# Never widen the budget.
